Before — a parser throws, so every caller needs a try/catch to avoid crashing on bad input:

```typescript
function parseAge(input: string): number {
  const n = Number(input);
  if (Number.isNaN(n) || n < 0) {
    throw new Error(`invalid age: ${input}`);
  }
  return n;
}
```

After — failure is part of the return type, so the caller can't ignore it without the type system noticing:

```typescript
type Result<T, E> = { ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; error: E };

function parseAge(input: string): Result<number, string> {
  const n = Number(input);
  if (Number.isNaN(n) || n < 0) {
    return { ok: false, error: `invalid age: ${input}` };
  }
  return { ok: true, value: n };
}

const result = parseAge(formInput);
if (!result.ok) {
  showFieldError(result.error);
} else {
  saveAge(result.value);
}
```

Parsing user input fails *often* and predictably — exactly the case where forcing every caller to branch on failure pays off.
